WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:04.780
All the Spider-Man films tend to feature the
same elements over and over again.

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:08.920
While a lot of it's worked well for the character
for about 60 years, you could go to the theater

00:00:08.920 --> 00:00:11.300
and play Spidey-Bingo pretty easily.

00:00:11.300 --> 00:00:14.360
Here are things that we see in every single
Spider-Man movie.

00:00:14.660 --> 00:00:18.560
Let's start with a gimme here: if you have
a nephew or niece, and you find out they've

00:00:18.570 --> 00:00:23.280
been bitten by a radioactive spider and developed
fantastic abilities like sticking to walls

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:27.180
or super strength, pack your bags and get
out of town as fast as you can.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:29.180
Otherwise, you're headed for disaster.

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:33.340
Admittedly, the death of his uncle is the
most important part of Spider-Man's origin

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:34.240
story, but still.

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:39.020
Watching an old man get shot starts to lose
its impact when you've seen it over and over,

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:42.480
which is especially problematic in the Sam
Raimi Spider-Man trilogy.

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.860
We get Uncle Ben's murder happening in the
first film, a flashback and tons of references

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:51.880
to it in Spider-Man 2, and a completely different
version of it that involves the sad-sack movie

00:00:51.880 --> 00:00:56.080
version of the Sandman making up a huge chunk
of the plot of Spider-Man 3.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:57.670
"Remember Ben Parker?

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:00.660
The old man you shot down in cold blood?!"

00:01:00.880 --> 00:01:05.640
It should probably be noted that this particular
brand of tragedy isn't limited to Ben.

00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:09.720
In Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse, Miles
Morales watches as his own uncle is killed

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:10.720
in front of him.

00:01:10.780 --> 00:01:15.680
As for the MCU, those movies have managed
to avoid even a mention of the late Ben Parker

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.120
so far, with only a couple of oblique references
to Peter's defining tragedy.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:24.240
Far From Home, however, is going to take place
in the aftermath of Tony Stark's death.

00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.360
He's not Peter's uncle by relation, but he's
definitely served as a father figure.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:32.020
And if ever there was someone in Peter's life
who fit the "weird uncle" archetype, it's

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:33.020
that guy.

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:33.780
"Hey May!

00:01:33.780 --> 00:01:34.600
How you doing?

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:35.500
What are you wearing?

00:01:35.500 --> 00:01:36.980
Something skimpy, I hope."

00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:42.520
One of the most interesting choices in Spider-Man's
design is that he's one of the few American

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:46.680
superheroes whose costume leaves him completely
covered from head to toe.

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:50.300
His mask doesn't even have a mouth, just a
pair of big opaque eyes.

00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:55.660
This works pretty well in comics, but in movies,
where actors use their faces to, you know,

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:57.720
act, it presents a bit of a problem.

00:01:57.720 --> 00:02:01.520
That's probably why the Spider-Man we see
in the movie theater seems like he'll do whatever

00:02:01.530 --> 00:02:05.830
he can to get out of that mask, regardless
of how it impacts his secret identity.

00:02:05.830 --> 00:02:09.120
Again, the Raimi trilogy is the biggest offender
in this regard.

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.140
Not only does Peter's mask get shredded by
villains with a frequency that makes you wonder

00:02:13.140 --> 00:02:17.000
just how many spares he has laying around,
he'll occasionally just whip the whole thing

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:18.000
off.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:22.380
Sure, it's a lot easier to tell a character's
struggling when you can see their face, but

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:26.760
eventually, people are going to wonder why
that kid who's always taking pictures of Spider-Man

00:02:26.760 --> 00:02:28.760
never seems to catch him without his mask
on.

00:02:28.980 --> 00:02:31.420
"...We won't tell nobody."

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:36.380
Along the same lines, Homecoming features
a recreation of the famous comic book scene

00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:39.280
where Spidey fights his way out from underneath
tons of rubble.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.480
In the comic, the mask stays on, but on the
big screen, Tom Holland's Spidey takes his

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:47.540
mask off to get some fresh air and keeps it
off so that we can see him powering through

00:02:47.540 --> 00:02:49.620
his superheroic feat of strength.

00:02:49.620 --> 00:02:53.860
Andrew Garfield's Peter Parker shows off his
face in Amazing Spider-Man to help calm down

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:55.060
a boy who's in danger.

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.870
It even extends to animated acting in Into
the Spider-Verse, where Spidey loses his mask

00:02:59.870 --> 00:03:04.280
just before he's killed by the Kingpin, and
Peter B. spends most of the movie unmasked…and

00:03:04.280 --> 00:03:06.000
in sweatpants.

00:03:06.340 --> 00:03:10.580
There are very few things that superhero movies
love as much as a good old-fashioned dose

00:03:10.580 --> 00:03:12.040
of crucifixion imagery.

00:03:12.040 --> 00:03:16.200
You might recall the scene in Man of Steel,
for instance, where our hero's ghostly space

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:20.370
father tells the son he sent to Earth that
he can "save" everyone, prompting Superman

00:03:20.370 --> 00:03:24.540
to stick his arms out and start hovering in
a pose that's pretty familiar to anyone who's

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:26.240
ever been inside a church.

00:03:26.240 --> 00:03:30.340
While that's the least subtle example of this
trope, Spider-Man's movies have even more

00:03:30.340 --> 00:03:31.320
of it in action.

00:03:31.320 --> 00:03:36.260
That's probably to be expected in a story
of great power and great responsibility, but

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:39.240
once you start noticing it, you'll find it
happens a lot.

00:03:39.240 --> 00:03:43.800
Spidey stopping the train in Spider-Man 2
is the most obvious instance, but when Peter

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:47.700
tries to hold the ferry together in Homecoming,
we get the same thing in a pretty similar

00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:48.520
shot.

00:03:48.520 --> 00:03:53.760
Spider-Man 3 even has an entire sequence that
takes place in a church, finally adding parasitic

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.440
alien symbiotes to classic Biblical imagery.

00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:01.420
Into the Spider-Verse actually manages to
parody the movies' cruci-fixation twice in

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:06.090
the first two minutes, including a shot showing
us Spidey bathed in rays of sunlight that

00:04:06.090 --> 00:04:08.320
give him just a tiny bit of a halo.

00:04:08.800 --> 00:04:13.180
One of the most compelling parts of Spider-Man's
story is the impact that his superheroic career

00:04:13.180 --> 00:04:15.500
has on his soap opera of a love life.

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.340
It's the source of endless drama, and it usually
gets pretty complicated.

00:04:19.340 --> 00:04:23.720
The same goes for the movies, every single
Spider-Man film has a love story right there

00:04:23.720 --> 00:04:27.280
at the core of its plot, with all the twists
and turns that you'd expect.

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:32.180
In the Raimi films, it was Peter's on-again,
off-again relationship with Mary Jane Watson,

00:04:32.190 --> 00:04:36.970
which starts out complicated and only gets
more tangled as the films continue, especially

00:04:36.970 --> 00:04:39.780
when Gwen Stacy shows up in Spider-Man 3.

00:04:39.780 --> 00:04:43.860
In Amazing Spider-Man, Gwen was free of a
love triangle, but that relationship had no

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:45.420
shortage of speed bumps.

00:04:45.420 --> 00:04:49.600
In the first film, Gwen's dad wanted to arrest
Spider-Man for his vigilante activity...

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:51.600
"I think he stands for what you stand for,
sir.

00:04:51.940 --> 00:04:53.620
Protecting innocent people from bad guys."

00:04:53.620 --> 00:04:55.040
"I stand for law and order, son.

00:04:55.050 --> 00:04:56.050
That’s what I stand for.

00:04:56.050 --> 00:04:57.510
I wear a badge.

00:04:57.510 --> 00:04:58.740
This guy wears a mask."

00:04:58.980 --> 00:05:03.400
...and in the second, a series of spats wound
up leading to Gwen's tragic death.

00:05:03.400 --> 00:05:07.220
Homecoming threw a different kind of wrench
into the gears with Peter's crush on classmate

00:05:07.220 --> 00:05:08.180
Liz.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:12.600
Not only did he have to deal with constantly
letting his friends down due to his arachnid

00:05:12.600 --> 00:05:16.780
activities, he also had to contend with the
reveal that her dad was a supervillain, who

00:05:16.780 --> 00:05:20.100
figured out Peter was Spider-Man literally
ten minutes after meeting him.

00:05:20.700 --> 00:05:22.920
"...Good old Spider-Man."

00:05:23.060 --> 00:05:27.460
Into the Spider-Verse complicates matters
further thanks to its dimension-hopping plot.

00:05:27.460 --> 00:05:31.520
We've got a Peter Parker who's dealing with
a divorce from Mary Jane, with whom he's clearly

00:05:31.520 --> 00:05:35.280
still in love, and a different Mary Jane who's
dealing with the death of her world's Peter

00:05:35.280 --> 00:05:36.280
Parker.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:40.380
And those are just the minor characters The
main romantic tension in that movie is between

00:05:40.380 --> 00:05:42.380
two people from different realities.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:43.900
That's a tough one.

00:05:43.900 --> 00:05:47.560
There's long distance relationships, and then
there's "my girlfriend?

00:05:47.560 --> 00:05:50.100
You don't know her, she lives on Earth-65."

00:05:50.620 --> 00:05:55.200
Spider-Man's roster of enemies is packed with
characters who are actually pretty sympathetic.

00:05:55.200 --> 00:05:59.600
Unlike, say, murderous clowns that only want
to watch the world burn, supervillains like

00:05:59.600 --> 00:06:04.100
the Lizard and the Sandman have motivations
that we can relate to, and come with a heaping

00:06:04.100 --> 00:06:07.660
helping of pathos that the movies are more
than willing to bring to the screen.

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:12.220
The Green Goblin in Spider-Man is the outlier
here, since it's pretty hard to be sympathetic

00:06:12.220 --> 00:06:16.500
when you're pumpkin-bombing an old lady's
bedroom while she's saying her bedtime prayers.

00:06:16.640 --> 00:06:19.220
"Deliver us…"

00:06:19.480 --> 00:06:20.480
"Finish it!

00:06:21.100 --> 00:06:22.160
Finish it!"

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:24.780
"...from evil!"

00:06:24.940 --> 00:06:29.720
Then again, even he has that scene where Norman
Osborn is being tormented by his own alternate

00:06:29.720 --> 00:06:30.600
personality.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:35.460
Doc Ock in Spider-Man 2 is shown to be a pretty
decent guy before he's driven to multi-armed

00:06:35.460 --> 00:06:40.610
criminal insanity, and in Amazing Spider-Man,
Curt Connors, the Lizard. just wants to regrow

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:41.800
his lost arm.

00:06:41.800 --> 00:06:45.560
The Vulture in Homecoming is maybe the least
sympathetic guy of the bunch.

00:06:45.780 --> 00:06:49.419
"I'll kill you and everybody you love."

00:06:49.780 --> 00:06:54.199
For all his talk about sticking up for the
little guy, Adrian Toomes drives a Jaguar

00:06:54.199 --> 00:06:58.370
and owns a massive house in New York City,
bought and paid for with the profits from

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:00.260
his high-tech arms dealing business.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:04.440
But still, his stated motivation of wanting
to take care of his family is hard to argue

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:09.420
with, and if nothing else, he keeps Peter's
identity a secret rather than seeking revenge.

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:12.040
Into the Spider-Verse goes the furthest with
this.

00:07:12.040 --> 00:07:16.320
Not only do we see the Prowler as a regular
guy hanging out with his favorite nephew,

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:18.780
but even the Kingpin has a shred of sympathy
in there.

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:22.860
Yes, he kills Spider-Man with his bare hands
and had a plan that might've destroyed all

00:07:22.860 --> 00:07:27.000
of reality, but it all comes from his desire
to see his wife and son again.

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:32.420
Their methods are bad, but their desires are
very human, and pretty far from world domination.

00:07:32.900 --> 00:07:36.980
In his first appearance, Spider-Man didn't
start out with his familiar Spider-Suit.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:41.960
Instead, his first outing after getting powers
was in a pretty terrible homemade ensemble,

00:07:41.960 --> 00:07:44.480
and that's an idea that the movies have kept
around.

00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:46.800
There's always a bad costume first.

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:51.220
In Spider-Man, it was a recreation of a scene
from the original comic where he fought a

00:07:51.229 --> 00:07:55.490
pro wrestler while wearing a sweatsuit and
ski mask tricked out with a stenciled spider

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:56.400
on the chest.

00:07:56.400 --> 00:08:00.260
In Amazing Spider-Man, it was a red mask with
dark wraparound shades.

00:08:00.270 --> 00:08:04.850
The MCU actually flipped the script a little
by starting Peter off, at least as far as

00:08:04.850 --> 00:08:09.729
the audience is concerned, in a comics-accurate
version of his famous red-and-blue suit, provided

00:08:09.729 --> 00:08:10.860
by Tony Stark.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:14.360
It's only at the end of Homecoming that he
goes back to his homemade hoodie.

00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:18.980
Into the Spider-Verse, however, takes the
cake on this particular trope, with bad costumes

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:20.240
all over the place.

00:08:20.240 --> 00:08:24.840
Not only does Peter spend most of the movie
rocking sweatpants and mismatched shoes, but

00:08:24.840 --> 00:08:27.860
Miles Morales doesn't get his costume until
the very end.

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:32.660
Before that, most of his spidery adventures
take place while he's wearing a cheap Spider-Man

00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:36.200
Halloween costume, purchased, of course, from
smilin' Stan Lee.

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:37.320
No refunds.

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:40.020
Incidentally, these are all just "bad" costumes.

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:43.280
If you're looking for the evil costumes, well,
that's a different guy.

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:44.980
"Outstanding!

00:08:45.180 --> 00:08:48.120
Now let's bite all their heads off and pile
them up in the corner."

00:08:48.380 --> 00:08:51.800
Spider-Man's life is pretty much defined by
tragedy.

00:08:51.800 --> 00:08:55.839
It's the core of his character, the "hard
luck hero" aspect that makes him so easy to

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:56.660
relate to.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.639
But if the movies go back to that well all
the time, it gets both boring and a little

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:01.540
too depressing.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:05.320
As much as we like watching him get knocked
down, it's always better to see him get back

00:09:05.320 --> 00:09:06.080
up again.

00:09:06.080 --> 00:09:10.360
That's why the movies always show us something
to remind us that being Spider-Man is actually

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:12.440
kind of awesome, and more than a little fun.

00:09:12.529 --> 00:09:17.459
"But after everything, I still love being
Spider-Man.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:18.900
I mean, who wouldn't?"

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:23.180
Spider-Verse gives us all kinds of moments
like this, from Spider-Man cutting an album

00:09:23.189 --> 00:09:27.529
of Christmas Carols to the extended comedy
of Miles accidentally getting his hand stuck

00:09:27.529 --> 00:09:28.900
to Gwen Stacy's hair.

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:33.320
Throw in some Karate Kid-style web-swinging
lessons, and it does a great job of showing

00:09:33.320 --> 00:09:36.020
us that having super-powers is actually pretty
cool.

00:09:36.020 --> 00:09:39.980
The Raimi movies do it, too, leaning into
zany comedy with Peter zipping around the

00:09:39.990 --> 00:09:42.449
city on his web-lines to deliver pizzas.

00:09:42.449 --> 00:09:46.680
He even looks like he's having fun with that
dance number, even if no one else is.

00:09:46.680 --> 00:09:51.440
Amazing Spider Man 2 takes that to the next
level, with Spidey getting to his graduation

00:09:51.440 --> 00:09:55.260
through a series of wild rides and slapstick,
crime-fighting hijinx.

00:09:55.260 --> 00:09:59.680
It's Homecoming that does this the best, though,
with the montage of Peter's after-school activities

00:09:59.680 --> 00:10:01.660
as the friendly neighborhood superhero.

00:10:01.660 --> 00:10:05.480
Not only is he excited to get out there and
start doing good, he gets a churro from a

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:09.779
nice lady that he eats while hanging out on
a fire escape, and even does a backflip when

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:11.580
he's asked to by members of the public.

00:10:11.580 --> 00:10:15.779
That's what sets Spider-Man apart from the
other superheroes: he's the guy who will stop

00:10:15.780 --> 00:10:18.440
and argue with you about who makes the best
sandwich.

00:10:18.560 --> 00:10:19.880
"Yeah, best sandwich in Queens…"

00:10:20.080 --> 00:10:21.319
"Sub Haven’s pretty good."

00:10:21.319 --> 00:10:22.389
"Eh, it's too much bread."

00:10:22.389 --> 00:10:23.180
"I like bread."

